findings.problem:
Isolated probe: 10,000 unlinks of 64-byte files. Each unlink is timed alongside a CPU spin of the same length on the same thread, as a preemption control. It varies:
- file state: fresh/unsynced, F_BARRIERFSYNC'd, or sync()+5 s settled;
- layout: 256-way fan-out versus flat, in sorted (reaper) or random order;
- Spotlight: indexed directory versus '.noindex';
- QoS: user-interactive, default, utility, background;
- a separate process doing F_FULLFSYNC every 50 ms.

Results at load 12:
- The spin control shows 0-1 stalls; unlinks show 7-13 stalls over 2 ms per 10,000, max 4.4-6.6 ms, in every combination.
- The stalls are regular in unlink count: about every 850-1,060 unlinks with fan-out and every 490-550 in a flat directory, often in pairs. A flat directory stalls about twice as often over the same per-iteration time, so the period follows the metadata dirtied, not a timer.

Eliminated causes:
- the benchmark's fresh, unsynced files (settled and barrier-synced files stall alike);
- directory syncs (none during the probe, and another process's F_FULLFSYNC adds no stalls);
- fan-out directory removal (the store never removes fan-outs, and flat is worse);
- Spotlight;
- QoS at low load.

Under load (30-90) CPU preemption dominates and depends strongly on QoS. Unlinks over 4 ms per 10,000 were 18 at default QoS against 136 at utility, which is the metadata executor's QoS, 333 at background and 4 at user-interactive.

Method caveat: getrusage's voluntary/involuntary switch counters cannot separate the two causes, because usleep counts as involuntary (control run3). The interleaved spin control is the discriminator used.

The benchmark's unlinkProfile (2,000 unlinks, about 2-4 stall periods) reports p50/p99, which by construction cannot show an event that happens once per 500-900 unlinks.

verdicts.reason:
I compiled and re-ran the standalone unlink probe (swiftc -O) on the repo volume, with the matrix and period subsets, at load 93 to 106 (the hunter's clean run was at load 12.9). Preemption is heavy at this load, so the spin control stalls too (5 to 23 over 2 ms at default QoS). Unlinks still stall about 1.5 to 3 times as often (23 to 48), and the stall indices show the claimed count periodicity:
- flat directory M4: 532, 1075, 2138, 2655, 3183, 3693 (about every 520 unlinks);
- fan-out .noindex M6: 1054, 2091, 3149, 4281, 5600 (about every 1,050-1,130), matching the hunter's 490-550 and 850-1,060;
- T1: 248, 1390, 2525, 3691.
At utility QoS (M8, M9), stalls rise to 322 to 342, with spins at 135 to 155, which confirms the preemption share under load.

I could not verify the low-load claims (QoS independence when quiet, Spotlight and F_FULLFSYNC elimination) at this load; they are plausible and consistent with ADR-050's own record of 5.2 to 10.8 ms single-unlink kernel stalls. This is root-cause detail for a known owner item (P-13, ADR-050 options A and B; option C is new). It is a SPEC 6 or quality-gates decision with no code defect, so it is non-blocking. The owner is WP-B (Blobs/BlobUnits.swift, BenchStorage.swift).
